export const DATA_40: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-047", num: 47, title: "Đề 047", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Lasting Appeal of Cartoons for Children", paragraphs: ["For generations, cartoons have been a beloved part of childhood. From Saturday morning television shows to streaming platforms available at any hour, animated programmes continue to capture the hearts and minds of young viewers around the world. But what exactly makes cartoons so appealing to children, and why has this fascination endured for so long?", "One of the most obvious reasons is the use of bright colours and exaggerated movement. Children are naturally drawn to vivid visuals, and animators take full advantage of this. Characters in cartoons move in ways that real people never could — they leap from great heights, spin like tops, and stretch their arms across an entire room. These impossible actions delight children precisely because they exist outside the rules of everyday life. The sense of wonder and surprise keeps young viewers engaged episode after episode.", "Beyond visual appeal, cartoons often feature simple, relatable storylines. A young character trying to make new friends, dealing with a bully, or learning to share a toy reflects the kinds of challenges children face in their own daily routines. When children see a cartoon character work through a problem that mirrors their own experience, they feel understood. This emotional connection is powerful. It gives children a safe way to explore complex feelings like jealousy, disappointment, or excitement without facing those emotions directly in real life.", "Many parents and educators also value cartoons for their educational content. Some of the most popular children's programmes are carefully designed to teach early literacy, basic mathematics, and even social skills. Shows that introduce letters and sounds, count objects, or demonstrate how to resolve conflicts politely are not merely entertainment — they serve as a gentle introduction to concepts children will need in school. Research has suggested that children who regularly watch educational cartoons tend to develop certain language skills more quickly than those who do not.", "However, not all cartoons carry such clearly educational messages. Many are designed purely for fun and laughter, and there is nothing wrong with that. Slapstick humour, in which characters fall over, bump into things, or make silly mistakes, has been a staple of children's animation for decades. This type of comedy allows children to laugh freely without worrying about hurting anyone's feelings. Laughter itself is an important part of childhood development, helping children to relax and feel joyful.", "The social dimension of watching cartoons should not be underestimated either. Children often discuss their favourite programmes with friends at school, re-enact scenes during playtime, or share opinions about which character is best. These conversations build communication skills and a sense of shared culture. Parents who watch cartoons together with their children also create opportunities for meaningful dialogue about the stories and values presented on screen.", "It is clear that cartoons occupy a special place in the lives of children for many different reasons. Whether they entertain, educate, or simply bring laughter, these animated worlds continue to grow and adapt with each new generation. As long as children are curious, imaginative, and in need of stories that speak to their world, cartoons will remain a permanent fixture of childhood."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Cartoons are more educational than other types of children's programmes.", "Cartoons remain popular with children for a variety of visual, emotional, and social reasons.", "Parents and educators should encourage children to watch cartoons every day.", "Children enjoy cartoons because they feature impossible physical actions."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what makes cartoon movements appealing to children?", options: ["They are slower and easier to follow than real human movements.", "They teach children how to exercise and stay active.", "They involve actions that go beyond what is possible in real life.", "They are similar to the movements children make during playtime."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one benefit of educational cartoons mentioned in the passage?", options: ["Children who watch them may develop certain language skills faster.", "They replace traditional classroom instruction for young learners.", "They are the most effective way to teach mathematics to children.", "They help children build physical coordination and motor skills."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the children watching the cartoon programme", "the animators who create the storylines", "the cartoon characters in the story", "the parents and educators who design cartoons"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is said about slapstick humour in cartoons?", options: ["It is considered too violent and is rarely used in modern cartoons.", "It was invented recently to replace educational content in programmes.", "It is most appreciated by older children who understand physical comedy.", "It has been a consistent feature of children's animation for many decades."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do cartoons contribute to children's social development?", options: ["By encouraging children to spend more time alone watching television", "By teaching children to compete with each other for the best opinions", "By replacing face-to-face interaction with digital storytelling", "By providing shared topics that children can discuss and connect over"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"designed\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["accidentally produced", "created with a specific purpose", "inspired by real events", "decorated"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as a reason for cartoons' appeal to children?", options: ["The opportunity for laughter and entertainment", "Bright colours and exaggerated movement", "Storylines that reflect children's everyday challenges", "The use of real actors to make characters more believable"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what can be inferred about children who watch cartoons with their parents?", options: ["They are more likely to prefer educational programmes over comedy ones.", "They usually find cartoons less enjoyable than children who watch independently.", "They may have more chances to talk about values and ideas with their parents.", "They tend to watch more hours of television than children who watch alone."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward cartoons for children?", options: ["Critical — the author believes cartoons distract children from more important activities.", "Positive — the author believes cartoons offer genuine value to children's development.", "Doubtful — the author questions whether cartoons truly benefit children.", "Neutral — the author presents both sides without expressing a clear position."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Biological Divide: Brain Development in Teens and Adults", paragraphs: ["For much of human history, adolescence was understood primarily as a social and emotional transition — the awkward years between childhood and adulthood. Yet scientific advances over the past three decades have revealed a far more complex picture. The teenage brain is not simply an immature version of the adult brain. It is, in fact, a distinct organ in a critical and dynamic phase of biological transformation, operating according to its own internal logic.", "The most significant structural difference lies in the prefrontal cortex, the region of the brain responsible for decision-making, impulse control, and long-term planning. In adults, this area functions as the brain's executive director, carefully weighing consequences before action is taken. In teenagers, however, the prefrontal cortex is still undergoing a process called myelination — the wrapping of nerve fibres in a fatty insulating layer that dramatically increases the speed and efficiency of neural communication. This process is not completed until the mid-twenties, which means that for most of adolescence, the brain's capacity for rational, measured judgement remains a work in progress.", "At the same time, the teenage brain experiences a surge in activity within the limbic system, the region most closely associated with emotion, reward-seeking, and social connection. Neuroscientists have found that adolescents show significantly stronger responses to pleasurable stimuli than either younger children or adults. The combination of a highly reactive emotional centre and an underdeveloped regulatory one creates what researchers sometimes describe as a neurological imbalance. This imbalance, while responsible for some of the reckless behaviour commonly associated with teenagers, also fuels their intense curiosity, creativity, and willingness to take risks that can lead to important discoveries.", "One particularly striking finding concerns the brain's sensitivity to social feedback. Teenagers are exquisitely attuned to the opinions of their peers in ways that adults are not. Neuroimaging studies have shown that when adolescents believe they are being evaluated or observed by people their own age, areas of the brain linked to self-awareness and emotional regulation light up far more intensely than in adult subjects completing the same tasks. This hypersensitivity is not a flaw — it reflects the evolutionary importance of social integration during a period when young humans must begin establishing identities independent of their families.", "Sleep patterns in teenagers also differ fundamentally from those of adults, and not merely because of lifestyle choices. Research has demonstrated that the adolescent brain undergoes a genuine biological shift in its circadian rhythm — the internal clock that governs sleep and waking. Melatonin, the hormone that signals the body to prepare for sleep, is released later in the evening in teenagers than in adults. As a result, asking a teenager to be fully alert and productive at 7 a.m. is, from a neurological standpoint, equivalent to asking an adult to perform the same tasks at 5 a.m. Several school districts that have experimented with later start times have reported meaningful improvements in student attendance, mood, and academic performance.", "Critics of adolescent brain research sometimes argue that these biological explanations risk providing teenagers with a convenient excuse for irresponsible behaviour. However, this concern misses a crucial point. Understanding the neurological basis of adolescent behaviour does not reduce accountability — it improves the quality of the support, education, and policy frameworks we build around young people. If we know that the teenage brain is biologically predisposed to prioritise immediate rewards over delayed ones, we can design environments that account for this tendency rather than simply punishing it.", "Ultimately, the growing body of research into adolescent brain development invites us to reconsider some of our most deeply held assumptions about young people. Rather than viewing teenagers as adults-in-waiting who simply lack experience, we might more accurately regard them as individuals navigating a unique and irreplaceable stage of neurological growth — one that, if supported wisely, lays the foundations for a lifetime of learning and resilience."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main argument of the passage?", options: ["The adolescent brain is biologically distinct and follows its own developmental logic.", "Teenagers behave irresponsibly because they have not yet gained enough life experience.", "Adults should be more lenient with teenagers because of the challenges they face.", "Modern neuroscience has proven that teenage behaviour cannot be changed or improved."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is the prefrontal cortex less effective in teenagers than in adults?", options: ["It receives less blood flow than other regions of the brain during the teenage years.", "The process of myelination in this region is not yet complete during adolescence.", "It produces excessive amounts of hormones that interfere with rational thinking.", "It is located in a different position in the teenage brain than in the adult brain."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["adolescents whose limbic systems are highly active", "neuroscientists who study reward-seeking behaviour", "researchers who describe the neurological imbalance", "younger children with less developed emotional responses"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do teenagers naturally tend to stay up later at night?", options: ["A hormonal shift in their biological clock delays the onset of sleepiness.", "Their social lives require them to stay active in the evenings.", "They use electronic devices excessively before going to sleep.", "They consume more caffeine than adults during the afternoon hours."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was one outcome observed in schools that introduced later start times?", options: ["Improvements were seen in student attendance, mood, and academic results.", "Students slept longer and showed improved physical health outcomes.", "Teachers reported less disruptive behaviour during morning classes.", "Parents expressed concern about the impact on after-school activities."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"myelination\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the formation of new emotional connections in the brain", "a process by which nerve fibres are coated to improve signal speed", "the gradual loss of nerve cells as a person ages", "a hormonal cycle that regulates mood during adolescence"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the author respond to critics who say neuroscience gives teenagers excuses for bad behaviour?", options: ["By agreeing that some teenagers do misuse scientific explanations", "By arguing that biological understanding enhances support rather than removing responsibility", "By ignoring the criticism and focusing only on positive aspects of teen behaviour", "By suggesting that courts should adopt neuroscience-based legal standards for teenagers"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following is NOT mentioned in paragraph 4 as a feature of the teenage brain's response to social situations?", options: ["A tendency to completely ignore the opinions of family members", "Heightened activity in areas related to self-awareness", "Greater emotional regulation activity compared to adult subjects", "Stronger reactions when peers are believed to be watching"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"predisposed\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["forced by external pressure", "prevented from making decisions", "naturally inclined in advance", "taught through repeated experience"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's overall perspective on adolescent brain research?", options: ["The author believes the research is still too limited to have practical applications.", "The author thinks teenagers should be fully responsible for their behaviour regardless of biology.", "The author is sceptical that brain science will ever change how society treats teenagers.", "The author supports using this knowledge to create better environments for young people."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "A Taste of Home: Vietnamese Cuisine in the Australian Landscape", paragraphs: ["When Vietnamese migrants began arriving in Australia in significant numbers following the end of the Vietnam War in 1975, they brought with them far more than personal belongings and memories. They carried a culinary tradition stretching back centuries — one rooted in the principles of balance, freshness, and regional diversity. Over the following decades, Vietnamese food would undergo a remarkable transformation in the Australian context, evolving from a little-known ethnic cuisine into one of the most widely recognised and celebrated food cultures in the country.", "The early years were marked by both hardship and resourcefulness. [1] Vietnamese families settling in suburban areas of Sydney, Melbourne, and Brisbane often found that key ingredients — fresh herbs like Vietnamese mint and perilla, specific cuts of pork, and fermented condiments such as mắm — were simply unavailable in mainstream supermarkets. [2] Rather than compromising on flavour, many families began growing herbs in backyard gardens or importing goods through nascent community networks. [3] In doing so, they inadvertently laid the groundwork for a network of Asian grocery stores and specialty suppliers that would eventually serve not only Vietnamese communities but the broader Australian public. [4]", "The rise of Vietnamese restaurants in Australian cities followed a predictable but meaningful arc . Initial establishments catered almost exclusively to Vietnamese migrants seeking comfort and familiarity. Gradually, however, word spread among non-Vietnamese Australians about the distinctive appeal of dishes like phở — a deeply aromatic beef broth simmered for hours and served with fresh herbs, lime, and chilli — and bánh mì, the Vietnamese baguette sandwich that fuses French colonial influence with Southeast Asian flavours. By the 1990s, Vietnamese restaurants had moved well beyond ethnic enclaves, establishing a presence in city centres, university districts, and suburban shopping strips across the country.", "What drove this mainstream acceptance was not merely novelty. Australian dining culture in the late twentieth century was undergoing a broader shift toward what food historians describe as \"multicultural gastronomy\" — an openness to ingredients, techniques, and traditions from non-Western culinary cultures. Vietnamese cuisine, with its emphasis on light broths, abundant vegetables, and minimal use of heavy cream or dairy, aligned naturally with the growing Australian interest in health-conscious eating. Nutritionists and food writers began drawing attention to the relatively low fat content and high herb density of traditional Vietnamese dishes, framing them as both delicious and nutritionally sound.", "Regional variation within Vietnamese cuisine also found expression in Australia, though unevenly. Southern Vietnamese cooking, characterised by its sweeter flavour profiles and generous use of fresh herbs, became the dominant style in most Australian Vietnamese restaurants — a reflection of the fact that the majority of early migrants came from southern Vietnam. Northern dishes, such as bún bò Huế, a spicier noodle soup from the central city of Huế, remained relatively obscure in mainstream Australian dining for many years, known primarily within tight-knit communities of migrants from that region. Only in the 2010s, with the rise of food media and culinary tourism, did these lesser-known regional specialties begin attracting wider attention.", "The role of second-generation Vietnamese Australians in reshaping the cuisine's public image cannot be overstated. Growing up between two cultures, these individuals often possessed both the technical knowledge of traditional cooking passed down within their families and the cultural fluency to communicate its value to a broader audience. Chefs, food bloggers, and restaurant owners from this generation began presenting Vietnamese cuisine not as an affordable, no-frills dining option — the reputation it had long carried in Australia — but as a sophisticated culinary tradition worthy of serious critical attention. Their work helped shift public perception and opened doors to a new generation of Vietnamese-Australian restaurants that combined traditional flavours with contemporary presentation.", "Today, Vietnamese food occupies a central position in the Australian culinary landscape. Phở shops operate alongside Italian cafés and Japanese ramen bars in the same inner-city laneways. Supermarket shelves carry fish sauce, rice paper, and fresh lemongrass as standard stock. Cooking classes dedicated to Vietnamese techniques attract participants from every demographic. In little more than four decades, a cuisine born of displacement and adaptation has become an inseparable part of what it means to eat in modern Australia — a testament not only to the resilience of Vietnamese migrants but to the capacity of food to build bridges across cultural divides."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Second-generation Vietnamese Australians are solely responsible for the success of Vietnamese food in Australia.", "Vietnamese cuisine gradually transformed from an unfamiliar immigrant tradition into a celebrated part of Australian food culture.", "Australian dining culture changed in the late twentieth century due to growing interest in health-conscious eating.", "Vietnamese migrants faced significant hardship in finding authentic ingredients in Australia."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did Vietnamese families respond to the lack of essential ingredients in mainstream supermarkets?", options: ["They cultivated herbs at home and built supply networks within their communities.", "They adapted their traditional recipes to use locally available Australian ingredients.", "They persuaded supermarket managers to stock Vietnamese products.", "They imported all their ingredients directly from Vietnam through official channels."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"acceptance\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a reluctant tolerance of unfamiliar customs", "agreement to follow a set of rules", "widespread embrace or approval by the general public", "the formal recognition given by government authorities"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did Vietnamese cuisine appeal to health-conscious Australians?", options: ["It was the least expensive cuisine available in most Australian cities.", "It featured light broths, plentiful vegetables, and low levels of fat and dairy.", "Its dishes were prepared using cooking methods that preserved more vitamins.", "It was promoted by the Australian government as a model of nutritious eating."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did Southern Vietnamese cooking styles dominate in Australian Vietnamese restaurants?", options: ["Southern dishes used ingredients that were easier to source in Australian markets.", "Northern Vietnamese cooking was considered too spicy for the average Australian palate.", "Australian diners specifically requested the sweeter flavour profiles of southern cuisine.", "Most of the early Vietnamese migrants to Australia originated from the south of Vietnam."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did second-generation Vietnamese Australians change the public image of Vietnamese food?", options: ["They lobbied the Australian government to include Vietnamese cuisine in school curricula.", "They replaced older Vietnamese restaurant owners by opening larger chain restaurants.", "They rebranded Vietnamese food as a sophisticated culinary tradition deserving serious recognition.", "They introduced entirely new dishes that had no connection to traditional Vietnamese cooking."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["nutritionists and food writers who reviewed Vietnamese restaurants", "traditional Vietnamese dishes described earlier in the paragraph", "health-conscious Australian diners seeking lighter food options", "the heavy creams and dairy products used in Western cooking"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"arc\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["an unexpected reversal of a previously held opinion", "a sharp disagreement between two opposing groups", "a gradual and structured course of development", "a curved physical structure used in architecture"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason northern Vietnamese dishes remained less known in Australia for many years?", options: ["Most early Vietnamese migrants came from southern rather than northern Vietnam.", "Northern dishes were mainly familiar within small, close-knit regional communities.", "Australian restaurant critics actively discouraged chefs from serving northern dishes.", "Food media and culinary tourism had not yet drawn wider public attention to them."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This shortage was not a minor inconvenience — it threatened to strip the food of the very qualities that made it meaningful.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Paradigm Shift: Re-evaluating the Rise of Homeschooling", paragraphs: ["For most of the twentieth century, homeschooling occupied the fringes of educational discourse — practised by a small minority of families motivated largely by religious convictions or deep dissatisfaction with state schooling. [1] It was, by mainstream standards, an eccentric choice, and the regulatory frameworks of most Western nations treated it accordingly: with suspicion, minimal support, and in some jurisdictions, outright prohibition. [2] Yet within the span of a single generation, homeschooling has been transformed from a marginal practice into a globally significant educational movement, one that now commands serious scholarly attention and, in many countries, formal legal protection. [3] What accounts for this shift, and what does the proliferation of home-based education reveal about the assumptions embedded in conventional schooling? [4]", "The growth of homeschooling across the anglophone world has been striking. In the United States, estimates suggest that the number of homeschooled children rose from approximately 850,000 in 1999 to over 3.3 million by 2016, with accelerated growth following the disruptions of the COVID-19 pandemic. Similar patterns have been documented in the United Kingdom, Canada, and Australia. Crucially, the demographic profile of homeschooling families has also shifted: where once the practice was concentrated among conservative religious communities, it now draws participants from across the political spectrum, including progressive urban parents disillusioned with standardised testing, families of children with neurodevelopmental conditions who find mainstream classrooms ill-suited to their needs, and high-achieving households seeking a more intellectually rigorous curriculum than local schools can offer.", "Proponents of homeschooling frequently invoke the concept of individualisation — the capacity to tailor pace, content, and pedagogical approach to the specific cognitive and emotional profile of a single child rather than a classroom of thirty. This argument has gained empirical traction. A growing body of research suggests that homeschooled children, on average, perform comparably or superiorly to their conventionally schooled peers on standardised academic assessments, though methodological concerns about sample self-selection complicate straightforward interpretation. More compelling, perhaps, are studies indicating that homeschooled students report higher levels of intrinsic motivation and lower rates of academic anxiety — outcomes that conventional schooling, with its reliance on external grading and competitive ranking, struggles to replicate.", "Critics, however, raise concerns that extend beyond academic attainment. The most persistent objection is socialisation: if children spend their formative years outside the institutional structures of school, how do they develop the social competencies required for participation in civic and professional life? Homeschooling advocates counter that this concern rests on a conflation of schooling with socialisation — as though the only legitimate site of social learning were a classroom. Many homeschooled children participate extensively in community sports, arts programmes, cooperative learning groups, and civic organisations. Yet the counterargument is not without force. The school environment exposes children to peers from diverse socioeconomic and cultural backgrounds in ways that self-selected homeschooling communities rarely replicate, and the significance of this exposure for the development of democratic tolerance is not easily quantified.", "A more structurally significant critique concerns equity. Homeschooling, in its most resource-intensive forms, presupposes a level of parental time, educational attainment, and financial flexibility that is unavailable to large portions of the population. A dual-income household with two full-time employed parents cannot, without substantial sacrifice, replicate the conditions that make homeschooling educationally effective. The proliferation of homeschooling among affluent and educated families therefore raises uncomfortable questions about whether this movement, whatever its merits for individual children, may inadvertently exacerbate existing educational inequalities by channelling engaged, resourced parents away from the public school system — the very institution most dependent on such families for advocacy and social capital.", "Technology has substantially altered the landscape of home-based education. Online curricula, virtual tutoring platforms, and synchronous learning communities have reduced the material barriers to homeschooling and expanded its accessibility well beyond its traditional demographic. Proponents argue that technology-enabled homeschooling represents a genuine democratisation of educational choice. Sceptics, however, note that access to broadband infrastructure and digital devices remains unevenly distributed, and that the pedagogical quality of online homeschooling resources varies enormously — a concern that regulatory frameworks have been slow to address.", "What the rise of homeschooling ultimately reveals is less a verdict on any particular educational method than a deeper interrogation of what schooling is for. If school is primarily a site for credentialling and sorting — producing measurable outputs for labour markets and higher education — then homeschooling can reasonably be evaluated by whether it achieves comparable outcomes at lower social cost. But if schooling serves a broader democratic function — cultivating shared civic identity, bridging social divides, and forming citizens capable of collective deliberation — then the calculus is considerably more complex. The homeschooling debate, at its most illuminating, does not merely ask whether children learn more at home. It asks what kind of society we are trying to build, and whether our educational institutions are genuinely designed to serve that purpose."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["To compare the socialisation outcomes of homeschooled children with those attending public schools", "To examine the rise of homeschooling and what it reveals about deeper assumptions underlying formal education", "To argue that governments should introduce stricter regulations to limit the growth of homeschooling", "To demonstrate that homeschooling consistently produces better academic results than conventional schooling"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, in what way has the demographic profile of homeschooling families changed in recent decades?", options: ["It has moved away from families with academically gifted children toward those with neurodevelopmental concerns.", "It has transitioned from families critical of standardised testing to those who actively support it.", "It has shifted from being dominated by affluent urban households to including more rural and working-class families.", "It has broadened from primarily religious communities to encompass a diverse range of motivations and backgrounds."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does research suggest about homeschooled children beyond academic performance?", options: ["They develop stronger collaborative skills due to small-group learning environments.", "They tend to show greater self-motivation and experience less anxiety about their studies.", "They are more likely to pursue higher education than conventionally schooled peers.", "They are better equipped for professional environments because of personalised instruction."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"individualisation\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the process of customising education to suit a specific child's profile", "the formal recognition of a student's unique cultural and linguistic background", "the prioritisation of personal achievement over collective academic goals", "the isolation of a learner from group-based educational activities"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["civic and professional communities that set social competency standards", "children educated outside conventional school institutions", "homeschooling advocates who support alternative socialisation", "critics who question the merits of homeschooling"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the author suggest that the growth of homeschooling among affluent families raises concerns about equity?", options: ["Affluent homeschooling communities often exclude families from lower socioeconomic backgrounds.", "Wealthy families tend to create homeschooling curricula that are too advanced for average children to access.", "Financial investment in homeschooling technology creates an uneven marketplace for educational software.", "Their withdrawal from the public school system may deprive it of influential advocates and resources."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern do sceptics raise about technology-enabled homeschooling?", options: ["Unequal access to technology and inconsistent quality of resources remain unresolved problems.", "Virtual tutoring communities create unhealthy competition between homeschooled children.", "Digital platforms make it harder for parents to monitor their children's academic progress.", "Online learning tools discourage children from developing independent thinking skills."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"exacerbate\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to make an already problematic situation considerably worse", "to create an entirely new category of social difficulty", "to draw public attention toward a neglected issue", "to reduce the severity of a pre-existing problem"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT stated as evidence supporting the effectiveness of homeschooling?", options: ["Studies suggest homeschooled children experience lower levels of academic anxiety.", "Homeschooled students perform at least as well as schooled peers on standardised assessments.", "Homeschooling has been formally endorsed by educational psychologists as the optimal learning model.", "Homeschooled students tend to demonstrate higher levels of intrinsic motivation toward learning."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'That consensus, however, has eroded with remarkable speed.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-130", num: 130, title: "Đề 130", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Studying Abroad: What to Expect", paragraphs: ["Every year, more and more students decide to leave their home country to study abroad. Some want a better education, while others are attracted by the chance to experience a new culture or improve their career opportunities after graduation. Whatever the reason, studying abroad has become a common choice for young people who want to broaden their horizons and gain skills that are hard to develop at home.", "Before students can study abroad, they must go through a long application process. First, they need to choose a country and a university that match their goals and budget. Then, they must prepare documents such as academic transcripts and English certificates like IELTS or TOEFL. Many students also apply for scholarships to help cover the cost of tuition. Once they are accepted by a university, the final step is applying for a student visa, which can sometimes take several weeks or even months to be approved.", "Money is one of the biggest concerns for students planning to study abroad. Tuition fees are different from country to country, and living costs such as rent, food, and transportation can add up quickly. Because of this, many international students take part-time jobs to support themselves while studying. Scholarships and financial aid programs can also make a big difference, allowing students from less wealthy families to study at universities they could not otherwise afford.", "Adjusting to life in a new country is not always easy. Many students feel homesick during their first few months abroad, especially if they are far from family and friends. Cultural differences, such as different social customs or ways of communicating, can also be confusing at first. Language barriers make daily tasks, like shopping or making friends, more difficult for students who are not yet confident in the local language. It usually takes time before students start to feel comfortable in their new environment.", "Fortunately, most universities understand these challenges and offer support to help international students adjust. International student offices provide guidance on housing, visas, and academic matters, while orientation programs introduce new students to campus life and connect them with other students in similar situations. These services make the transition smoother and help students build a support network away from home.", "Despite the challenges, most students who study abroad describe the experience as rewarding. They often return home with stronger language skills, greater independence, and a wider view of the world. Education experts encourage students who are considering studying abroad to plan carefully, from choosing the right program to preparing financially, so that they can make the most of this valuable opportunity."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage argues that studying abroad is too difficult and expensive for most students.", "The passage focuses only on the financial costs of studying in a foreign country.", "The passage mainly explains how to apply for a student visa in different countries.", "The passage describes why students study abroad, the process involved, the challenges they face, and the support and benefits that come with the experience."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what must students do before applying for a student visa?", options: ["They must first choose a university, prepare documents such as transcripts and English certificates, and apply for scholarships.", "They must join an orientation program organized by the university.", "They must complete their first semester of study abroad successfully.", "They must first find a part-time job to prove they can support themselves."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do many international students take part-time jobs?", options: ["To gain work experience required for graduation.", "To qualify for a scholarship from their university.", "To help support themselves and manage the cost of living while studying.", "To meet the visa requirements set by their host country."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what makes adjusting to life abroad difficult for many students?", options: ["The long amount of time it takes to receive a student visa.", "Feelings of homesickness, cultural differences, and language barriers.", "The high cost of university tuition in most countries.", "A lack of interest in meeting new people from other cultures."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do students often gain after completing their studies abroad?", options: ["A full refund of their tuition fees from the university.", "Automatic citizenship in the country where they studied.", "A guaranteed high-paying job in their home country.", "Stronger language skills, greater independence, and a wider view of the world."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a service that universities provide to international students?", options: ["Support for academic matters through international student offices.", "Guidance on housing and visa matters.", "Orientation programs that introduce new students to campus life.", "Free round-trip flights home during university holidays."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"broaden\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Repeat", "Expand", "Limit", "Ignore"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"transition\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Delay", "Decision", "Change", "Payment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"These services\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["Part-time jobs available to students", "English certificates like IELTS or TOEFL", "International student offices and orientation programs", "Scholarships and financial aid programs"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the timing of a student's financial planning?", options: ["Financial planning is unnecessary if a student is accepted into a university.", "Students should wait until after receiving their visa to think about tuition and living costs.", "Because the visa process can take weeks or months and living costs add up quickly, students likely need to plan their finances well before they actually depart.", "Universities automatically handle all financial planning for international students."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Sleep and the Foundations of Human Health", paragraphs: ["For much of the twentieth century, sleep was widely regarded as a passive state in which the body and brain simply shut down to conserve energy. Contemporary research has overturned this assumption, revealing sleep to be an active and highly regulated process essential to nearly every physiological system. Far from being a period of inactivity, the sleeping brain cycles through distinct stages, each associated with specific patterns of neural activity and distinct restorative functions.", "One of the clearest links between sleep and health concerns memory consolidation . During deep, slow-wave sleep, the brain appears to replay and strengthen neural connections formed during waking hours, effectively transferring information from short-term to long-term storage. Sleep-deprived individuals consistently perform worse on memory tasks than their well-rested counterparts, even when the amount of information initially learned is identical. This suggests that adequate sleep is not merely restful but is an active component of the learning process itself.", "Sleep also plays a substantial role in regulating metabolic and hormonal balance. Insufficient sleep has been associated with elevated levels of ghrelin, a hormone that stimulates appetite, alongside reduced levels of leptin, which signals fullness. Over time, this hormonal imbalance may contribute to overeating and weight gain, independent of changes in diet or physical activity. Researchers have also linked chronic sleep restriction to impaired glucose tolerance, a risk factor commonly associated with the development of type 2 diabetes.", "The cardiovascular system, too, appears sensitive to sleep quality and duration. [1] Large-scale population studies have found that individuals who sleep fewer than six hours per night, on average, face a measurably higher risk of hypertension and cardiovascular disease compared with those who sleep seven to eight hours. [2] Researchers caution, however, that establishing a purely causal relationship remains difficult, since people who sleep poorly may also share other risk factors, such as elevated stress or irregular working hours, that independently affect cardiovascular health. [3] Even so, the association is consistent enough across multiple studies that many physicians now consider sleep duration a meaningful factor in cardiovascular risk assessment. [4]", "Beyond its physical effects, sleep is closely tied to emotional regulation and mental health. The amygdala, a brain region involved in processing emotional responses, becomes markedly more reactive following sleep deprivation, while its connectivity to the prefrontal cortex—the region responsible for rational judgment—weakens. This altered connectivity may help explain why sleep-deprived individuals often report heightened irritability and difficulty managing stress. Longitudinal studies have further found that chronic sleep problems frequently precede, rather than merely accompany, the onset of mood disorders such as depression.", "Given this growing body of evidence, sleep researchers increasingly argue that sleep deserves to be treated as a pillar of health alongside diet and exercise, rather than as a lifestyle factor that can be sacrificed without meaningful consequence. Public health campaigns in several countries have begun incorporating sleep guidance into broader wellness initiatives, reflecting a shift away from viewing sleep loss as a minor inconvenience and toward recognizing it as a measurable contributor to chronic disease risk.", "Nevertheless, translating this research into individual behavior change has proven difficult. Modern work schedules, artificial lighting, and constant access to digital devices all work against the body's natural sleep-wake rhythms, and public awareness of sleep's importance does not always translate into improved sleep habits. Researchers note that addressing this gap will likely require structural changes—such as adjusted school and work start times—rather than relying solely on individual willpower."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage claims that public health campaigns have already solved the problem of widespread sleep deprivation.", "The passage explains how sleep actively supports memory, metabolic, cardiovascular, and emotional health, and why translating this understanding into behavior change remains difficult.", "The passage argues that sleep deprivation is the leading cause of type 2 diabetes worldwide.", "The passage focuses mainly on describing the biological stages of sleep in technical detail."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role does deep, slow-wave sleep play in memory?", options: ["It appears to reinforce recently formed neural connections, effectively moving information from short-term into long-term storage.", "It has no measurable effect on how well people perform on memory tasks.", "It prevents the brain from forming any new memories until the following day.", "During deep, slow-wave sleep, the brain appears to replay and strengthen neural connections formed during waking hours."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how might insufficient sleep contribute to weight gain over time?", options: ["It reduces ghrelin levels while increasing leptin levels, directly causing the body to burn fewer calories.", "It has been shown to increase physical activity levels, which paradoxically leads to greater hunger.", "It raises appetite-stimulating ghrelin and lowers fullness-signaling leptin, an imbalance that may lead to overeating even without changes in diet or activity.", "It only affects glucose tolerance, with no documented connection to appetite or hormone levels."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the relationship between sleep duration and cardiovascular disease, according to researchers?", options: ["No meaningful relationship between sleep duration and cardiovascular health has been found in large-scale studies.", "There is a consistent association between shorter sleep and higher cardiovascular risk, but researchers caution that other shared factors make a purely causal link difficult to establish with certainty.", "Researchers have concluded with certainty that short sleep duration directly causes cardiovascular disease in all populations.", "Individuals who sleep fewer than six hours per night, on average, face a measurably higher risk of hypertension and cardiovascular disease."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence associated with insufficient sleep?", options: ["A permanent and irreversible loss of long-term memory in nearly all individuals.", "Increased reactivity of the amygdala and weakened connectivity to the prefrontal cortex.", "Elevated ghrelin levels and reduced leptin levels.", "A higher likelihood of developing hypertension and cardiovascular disease."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"consolidation\" , as used in the context of paragraph 2 describing how memories are strengthened and stored, is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Reinforcement", "Measurement", "Interruption", "Prevention"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"translate\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Explain clearly", "Result in", "Interfere with", "Convert into another language"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The phrase \"this gap\" in paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["The gap between short-term and long-term memory storage", "The difference between school and work start times", "The gap between growing awareness of sleep's importance and people's actual sleep habits", "The difference between artificial lighting and natural sleep-wake rhythms"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what can be inferred about why chronic sleep problems might precede mood disorders?", options: ["Because weakened connectivity between the amygdala and prefrontal cortex may leave emotional responses less regulated by rational judgment over time, mood disorders are therefore proven to be caused exclusively by poor sleep.", "Because weakened connectivity between the amygdala and prefrontal cortex may leave emotional responses less regulated by rational judgment over time, this could plausibly contribute to the eventual onset of mood disorders.", "The amygdala shrinks permanently after a single night of poor sleep, directly causing depression.", "Sleep-deprived individuals show no measurable change in emotional reactivity, according to the passage."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Similar patterns have been documented in populations across different countries and age groups, strengthening confidence in the overall trend.'", options: ["[3]", "[2]", "[1]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Transformation of Retail in the Digital Age", paragraphs: ["Retail has undergone a fundamental restructuring over the past three decades, as commerce conducted primarily through physical storefronts has increasingly given way to transactions mediated by digital platforms. Amazon, which began in 1994 as an online bookseller operating out of a garage, offers a particularly instructive case study: its evolution into a sprawling global marketplace selling nearly every conceivable product category illustrates not merely the growth of one company but the broader trajectory of an industry reorganized around data, logistics, and algorithmic mediation rather than physical proximity to customers.", "Central to this reorganization is the recommendation algorithm, a system that analyzes a customer's browsing history, past purchases, and behavioral patterns to surface products deemed likely to generate a sale. Retailers champion these systems as a genuine convenience, arguing that they reduce the cognitive burden of sorting through vast inventories. Critics counter that the same mechanism, by consistently reinforcing a narrow band of previously expressed preferences, may gradually narrow rather than expand the range of products a consumer is ever exposed to—a dynamic sometimes termed a filter bubble, borrowed from analogous critiques of social media content curation.", "Consumer expectations regarding delivery speed have shifted just as dramatically. What was once considered acceptable—delivery within one to two weeks—has been replaced by an expectation of next-day or even same-day fulfillment, a standard made possible only through extensive networks of regional warehouses, commonly termed fulfillment centers, positioned strategically to minimize the physical distance between inventory and customer. This logistical transformation has not been without controversy: labor advocates have raised sustained concerns about the pace demanded of warehouse workers, arguing that productivity quotas calibrated to meet rapid delivery promises can come at a measurable cost to worker wellbeing, a claim that several companies, including Amazon, have publicly disputed while nonetheless adjusting some internal practices in response.", "The effect of large online marketplaces on small, independently owned retailers resists any simple characterization. On one hand, competition from platforms capable of offering lower prices, broader selection, and faster delivery has measurably eroded the customer base of many local shops, particularly in categories such as books, electronics, and household goods where price comparison is straightforward. On the other hand, these same marketplaces have created a distribution channel through which small sellers, once limited to local foot traffic, can reach customers across an entire country or beyond, provided they can navigate the platform's fee structures and algorithmic visibility requirements—a trade-off that benefits some independent sellers considerably more than others.", "Alongside these structural shifts, the psychology of consumer behavior has itself been reshaped. The near-instant availability of products, combined with one-click purchasing mechanisms that remove most of the friction traditionally associated with a buying decision, appears to have measurably increased impulse purchasing relative to earlier retail formats. Simultaneously, the prevalence of customer reviews has introduced a countervailing form of deliberation, as prospective buyers increasingly consult aggregated ratings before completing a purchase—a behavior that did not exist at comparable scale in pre-digital retail environments and that some researchers argue has partially offset the impulsivity encouraged by frictionless checkout.", "These conveniences carry an underexamined cost in the form of data collection. Every browsing session, search query, and completed purchase generates data that retailers use not only to refine recommendations but also to construct detailed behavioral profiles, which are frequently shared with or sold to third-party advertisers. Regulatory responses have varied considerably by jurisdiction: the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation imposes comparatively strict consent and disclosure requirements, whereas regulatory frameworks in many other regions remain considerably more permissive, leaving consumers there with less visibility into, and less control over, how their data is used.", "Taken together, these developments suggest that the future of retail is unlikely to resemble either the purely physical marketplaces of the past or a wholly digital alternative, but rather some hybrid arrangement in which physical and digital channels remain interdependent. Whether this arrangement ultimately serves consumers, workers, and small businesses equitably, or primarily concentrates advantage among the largest platforms, remains a matter of considerable debate among economists and policymakers alike."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage argues that Amazon's business practices are primarily responsible for the decline of small retailers worldwide.", "The passage concludes that physical retail stores will disappear entirely within the next decade.", "The passage focuses mainly on describing the technical design of recommendation algorithms used by online retailers.", "The passage traces the broader restructuring of retail around data, logistics, and algorithms, examining both the benefits and the unresolved costs this shift has produced for consumers, workers, and small businesses."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do retailers and critics differ in their assessment of recommendation algorithms?", options: ["Both retailers and critics agree that recommendation algorithms have no meaningful effect on what consumers ultimately purchase.", "Critics argue recommendation algorithms are ineffective at generating sales, while retailers insist they primarily protect consumer privacy.", "Retailers view these systems primarily as a way to reduce the effort of browsing large inventories, while critics contend the same mechanism may narrow, rather than broaden, the range of products consumers encounter over time.", "Retailers champion these systems as a genuine convenience, arguing that they reduce the cognitive burden of sorting through vast inventories."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has enabled the shift toward faster delivery expectations, and what controversy has it produced?", options: ["Same-day delivery became possible only after companies eliminated warehouse labor entirely through automation.", "Delivery speed increased primarily because customers began ordering smaller quantities of goods per transaction.", "Extensive networks of fulfillment centers positioned near customers have made rapid delivery possible, but this has raised concerns about the productivity pressures placed on warehouse workers.", "Labor advocates have raised sustained concerns about the pace demanded of warehouse workers."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the overall effect of large online marketplaces on small, independent retailers?", options: ["Small retailers have universally benefited from large online marketplaces, with no reported negative consequences.", "Large marketplaces have had no measurable impact on small, independently owned retailers.", "The effect is mixed: marketplaces have hurt some local shops through price competition, while simultaneously giving other small sellers broader reach, though not all sellers benefit equally.", "Competition from platforms capable of offering lower prices, broader selection, and faster delivery has measurably eroded the customer base of many local shops."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do data regulation approaches differ across regions?", options: ["The European Union enforces comparatively strict consent and disclosure rules, while many other regions maintain more permissive frameworks that leave consumers there with less control over their data.", "Every country outside the European Union has adopted regulations that are stricter than the GDPR.", "The European Union's General Data Protection Regulation imposes comparatively strict consent and disclosure requirements.", "Data regulation has become identical worldwide following the introduction of the GDPR."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor contributing to increased impulse purchasing in online retail?", options: ["The near-instant availability of products.", "The claim that all online shoppers now make every purchase impulsively, without exception.", "One-click purchasing mechanisms that remove friction from the buying decision.", "Personalized advertisements that follow shoppers across every website they visit."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"eroded\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Expanded", "Documented", "Protected", "Diminished"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"countervailing\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Accelerating", "Reinforcing", "Counterbalancing", "Duplicating"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"a claim\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["The assertion that productivity quotas tied to fast delivery may come at a cost to warehouse workers' wellbeing.", "The assertion that companies have universally agreed to raise warehouse wages.", "The claim that fulfillment centers are always located far from customers.", "The idea that same-day delivery is technically impossible for most retailers."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view on who ultimately benefits from the digital retail transformation?", options: ["The author argues explicitly that large platforms are the sole beneficiaries of digital retail, with no offsetting advantages for anyone else.", "The author suggests that the effects are genuinely mixed and unevenly distributed, and deliberately avoids concluding definitively whether the largest platforms or smaller participants benefit more.", "The author claims that this question has already been conclusively settled by economists and policymakers.", "The author suggests that the effects are genuinely mixed and unevenly distributed, and ultimately concludes that small businesses benefit more than large platforms in nearly every case."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Open-Source Software and the Limits of Collective Governance", paragraphs: ["Open-source software, distributed under licenses that permit anyone to inspect, modify, and redistribute its underlying code, occupies a curious position within contemporary technology: simultaneously heralded as a triumph of decentralized collaboration and quietly dependent, in many of its most consequential instances, on a strikingly narrow base of sustained volunteer labor. This tension between celebrated openness and precarious maintenance has become increasingly visible as critical infrastructure software—code embedded, often invisibly, within countless commercial products—has been revealed to rest on the unpaid efforts of a handful of contributors.", "The economic logic underlying open-source contribution does not map neatly onto conventional labor markets. Some contributors are compensated indirectly, employed by companies that rely on the software they help maintain and thus have a commercial incentive to fund their labor. Others contribute without any financial return whatsoever, motivated by reputational incentives, intellectual satisfaction, or a sense of obligation to a community from which they have themselves benefited. It would be a mistake, however, to assume that this diversity of motivation guarantees resilience; a project sustained primarily by unpaid enthusiasm may not necessarily persist once the personal circumstances of its few key maintainers change.", "Governance structures across open-source projects vary considerably, ranging from loosely organized communities with no formal decision-making hierarchy to foundations with elected boards, formal voting procedures, and codified contribution guidelines. Neither model has demonstrated unambiguous superiority. Informal governance can adapt quickly and lower the barrier to contribution, yet it may also concentrate effective authority in whichever individuals happen to be most active, regardless of whether that concentration was ever explicitly sanctioned by the broader community. Formal governance, conversely, can distribute authority more transparently, but its procedural requirements sometimes slow decision-making to a degree that discourages the very contributors it is meant to empower.", "Security has emerged as a particularly fraught dimension of this governance question. [1] Because open-source code is, by definition, visible to anyone, proponents have long argued that vulnerabilities are more likely to be identified and patched quickly, a principle sometimes summarized as 'many eyes make bugs shallow.' [2] This assumption does not necessarily hold uniformly across projects, however; visibility alone does not guarantee scrutiny, and several major vulnerabilities have persisted undetected for years in widely used libraries maintained by only one or two individuals, suggesting that theoretical exposure to review is not equivalent to review actually occurring in practice. [3] Consequently, some security researchers have begun to argue that project popularity, as measured by usage, should not be conflated with the likelihood that its code has been rigorously examined. [4]", "Corporate involvement in open-source ecosystems introduces further complexity that resists straightforward evaluation. Companies that contribute financial resources, engineering time, or infrastructure to open-source projects arguably strengthen their long-term viability considerably; at the same time, such involvement may subtly reorient a project's priorities toward the interests of its largest corporate sponsors, potentially at the expense of use cases favored by the broader volunteer community that does not share those commercial incentives. Whether this represents a net benefit or a quiet erosion of community autonomy likely depends heavily on the specific governance safeguards a given project has managed to establish beforehand.", "Licensing choices further complicate this picture, as they determine not merely legal permissions but, indirectly, the economic incentives available to different classes of participants. Permissive licenses impose relatively few restrictions on how derivative works may be used, including by companies that build proprietary products atop open-source foundations without contributing improvements back to the original project. Copyleft licenses, by contrast, typically require that derivative works remain similarly open, a requirement that some developers view as essential to preserving the communal character of a project and others view as a deterrent to the very commercial adoption that might otherwise fund its maintenance.", "Taken together, these dynamics suggest that the sustainability of any given open-source project cannot be reliably inferred merely from its popularity, its licensing model, or the visibility of its code to potential reviewers considered in isolation. Rather, sustainability appears to emerge, when it does, from a frequently precarious alignment of funding mechanisms, governance safeguards, and a sufficient density of engaged contributors—an alignment that, evidence suggests, may not necessarily persist merely because it happened to hold at some earlier point in a project's history.", "This has prompted growing calls, from both within the open-source community and from policymakers concerned about software supply-chain risk, to treat the maintenance of critical open-source infrastructure as a matter of collective responsibility rather than an incidental byproduct of individual goodwill. Whether such calls will translate into durable funding structures, or whether they will instead prove, as similar proposals arguably have in the past, more persuasive in principle than in sustained practice, remains an open question that the passage does not purport to resolve."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage focuses primarily on comparing the legal terms of different open-source licenses.", "The passage examines the tension between open-source software's celebrated collaborative ideal and the often fragile economic, governance, and security realities underlying its maintenance.", "The passage concludes that corporate involvement in open-source projects should be banned to preserve community autonomy.", "The passage argues that open-source software is inherently more secure than proprietary software in every case."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the diversity of contributor motivations not necessarily ensure a project's long-term sustainability?", options: ["It would be a mistake, however, to assume that this diversity of motivation guarantees resilience.", "Diverse motivation among contributors has been shown to eliminate any risk of project abandonment entirely.", "Because unpaid contributors are motivated by varied factors rather than direct compensation, a project may still falter if the personal situations of a few essential maintainers change.", "Because all open-source contributors are paid indirectly by companies, sustainability is essentially guaranteed regardless of individual circumstances."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what trade-off exists between informal and formal governance structures in open-source projects?", options: ["Formal governance structures have been shown to eliminate all barriers to contribution in every documented case.", "Informal governance can adapt quickly and lower the barrier to contribution, yet it may also concentrate effective authority in whichever individuals happen to be most active.", "Informal structures allow faster adaptation but risk unsanctioned concentration of influence, while formal structures distribute authority more transparently at the cost of slower decision-making.", "Neither governance structure has any effect on how quickly a project can make decisions."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the passage suggest about the relationship between a project's popularity and the thoroughness of its security review?", options: ["Popular projects are always more secure than obscure ones because more people are actively reviewing their code.", "Widespread use does not guarantee that code has actually been rigorously reviewed, since theoretical visibility to potential reviewers is not the same as review actually taking place.", "Security vulnerabilities in open-source software have been almost entirely eliminated due to widespread code visibility.", "Several major vulnerabilities have persisted undetected for years in widely used libraries maintained by only one or two individuals."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the fundamental trade-off between permissive and copyleft licenses, according to the passage?", options: ["Permissive licenses may encourage broader commercial adoption but allow companies to build on the code without contributing back, while copyleft licenses preserve openness but may deter some commercial use that could otherwise fund maintenance.", "Both license types have been shown to produce identical outcomes for project funding and community contribution.", "Permissive licenses require all derivative works to remain open, while copyleft licenses impose no restrictions whatsoever.", "Copyleft licenses, by contrast, typically require that derivative works remain similarly open."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a factor contributing to the sustainability of an open-source project?", options: ["A sufficient density of engaged, ongoing contributors.", "The claim that popularity alone is always sufficient to guarantee a project's long-term survival.", "Governance safeguards established before corporate involvement deepens.", "Funding mechanisms that support key maintainers over time."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"conflated\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Documented", "Reduced", "Distinguished", "Equated"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"precarious\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Deliberate", "Profitable", "Widespread", "Unstable"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"such involvement\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["The volunteer community's decision to reject corporate funding entirely.", "Corporate contribution of financial resources, engineering time, or infrastructure to open-source projects.", "The process of choosing between permissive and copyleft licenses.", "The formal voting procedures used by some open-source foundations."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on the passage as a whole, what can be inferred about the author's stance on whether growing calls to treat open-source maintenance as a collective responsibility will succeed?", options: ["The author suggests that success is plausible only if funding, governance, and contributor density remain aligned over time, but deliberately withholds a confident prediction that such alignment will hold, given that similar proposals have not always translated into lasting practice.", "The author argues that individual goodwill alone has always been, and will remain, a fully sufficient basis for maintaining critical open-source infrastructure.", "The author states definitively that these calls have already produced durable, well-funded structures across the open-source ecosystem.", "The author suggests that success is plausible only if funding, governance, and contributor density remain aligned over time, and predicts confidently that these calls will fail based on the historical pattern described."], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
];
